@* The mark for a destination, in one place.

   WHY A COMPONENT AND NOT INLINE SVG
   ==================================================================================
   The same five destinations appear in two navs -- the tab bar on a phone, the row of buttons on a
   desktop -- and both are supposed to be the same nav at two widths. NavMenu's own note commits to
   that: "a nav whose contents reshuffle between a laptop and a phone is one you have to re-learn."
   The contents already matched. The marks did not, because they were inline in the tab bar and
   absent from the desktop row.

   Copying the path data across would be the mistake PackMark was written to end: "path data copied
   into two files is path data that disagrees with itself after the first tweak." So the glyphs live
   here and both navs read from them, and the booster pack keeps coming from PackMark because it is
   in the wordmark too.

   currentColor throughout, no literal colours: the same drawing has to sit in body ink on a pale
   bar, white on a filled active button, and the accent on a selected tab, without any of the three
   saying anything about colour.

   A 24 viewBox and a 1.8-1.9 stroke on every mark, so they carry the same weight as each other at
   the same rendered size. The caller sets that size: 22px beside a tab label, 15px beside a nav
   button's word, where the mark is a hint at the word rather than the thing you aim at. *@

<svg class="@Class" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="@Size" height="@Size"
     focusable="false" aria-hidden="true">
    @switch (For)
    {
        @* Four panes: the grid the page opens as, which is the only one of these a reader has
           already seen full-size before they see the mark. *@
        case "collection":
            <rect x="3" y="3" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            <rect x="13.5" y="3" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            <rect x="3" y="13.5" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            <rect x="13.5" y="13.5" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            break;

        @* Four bars at four heights: one panel per set, each at its own standing, so the shape
           says "several things, each part-done". Deliberately not a percentage ring, which says
           one figure -- and one figure is what that page refuses to give, because the sets do not
           total. *@
        case "progress":
            <path d="M4 20V10M9.5 20V5M15 20v-8M20.5 20v-5"
                  fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" />
            break;

        @* A box seen in three-quarter view: a pack of five, which is what the page ranks. *@
        case "packs":
            <path d="M4 8.5 12 4l8 4.5v7L12 20l-8-4.5z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            <path d="M4 8.5 12 13l8-4.5M12 13v7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            break;

        case "more":
            <path d="M4 7h16M4 12h16M4 17h16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" />
            break;
    }
</svg>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Which destination's mark to draw: "collection", "progress", "packs" or "more".
    ///
    /// A string rather than an enum because the callers are markup and the value is written once
    /// per nav entry beside the href it belongs to -- an enum would put the two spellings of one
    /// destination on different lines. An unknown name draws nothing rather than throwing: a nav
    /// with a missing glyph is a nav, and a nav that threw would take the whole shell down.
    ///
    /// The booster pack is NOT here. It comes from <see cref="PackMark"/>, which the wordmark and
    /// the centre tab already share.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string For { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// Rendered size in px. 22 beside a tab label, where the tab is the target; 15 beside a
    /// desktop nav button's word, where the word is.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Size { get; set; } = 22;

    /// <summary>Class on the svg, so a caller can size it from the stylesheet instead.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
}
